@prefix review: <http://example.org/agora/review#> .
@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/agora/sensing#> .
@prefix water: <http://example.org/agora/water#> .
@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/agora/mqtt#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix ssn: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix market: <http://example.org/agora/market#> .
@prefix scaling: <http://example.org/agora/scaling#> .
@prefix unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .

#  A ratified world, v1 — the SMALLEST one that produces a working agent.
#
#  One bus, one subject, one board, one agent. No market, no supplier, no valves, and
#  therefore no economy at all: this world has nothing to allocate, so nobody bids and nobody
#  hosts. It is the whole of what is needed to perceive something.
#
#  What it is FOR: bringing a real board up, and integration tests that have no business
#  starting a market. It is also the clearest demonstration of the central claim — nothing
#  here says what the agent can DO. It is wired to a sensor and to nothing else, and
#  derivation gives it sensing:Subscribing and nothing else. Seed world/society instead, without
#  touching the board or the agent, and the very same hardware joins a market.
#
#  The device ids and channels are deliberately IDENTICAL to world/society, so one flashed
#  board works in either world. Which world is in the store decides what the agent becomes.
#
#  See knowledge/domain/world.md, knowledge/decisions/world-graph.md.

##########  The bus everyone speaks over  ##########

ag:local_bus a mqtt:MessageBus ;
    ag:localId "local_bus" ;
    mqtt:brokerHost "localhost" ;
    mqtt:brokerPort 1884 ;
    # Agents connect here and prove who they are with a certificate; the boards keep the
    # plaintext port above. One bus, one ACL, two ways of authenticating.
    mqtt:brokerTlsPort 8884 .

##########  World version  ##########

#  Who ratified it. A REFERENCE, never a declaration: the world says which user stands behind
#  these files and says nothing else about them — no name, no key, no type. A user is
#  installation-level (AGENTS.md rule 3) and an agent is given only its world, so the URI is an
#  identifier it never resolves, exactly as `mqtt:brokerHost` names a host it never introspects.
#  Sovereign is the ROLE that user held HERE — stated, not assumed, because another user may
#  operate what this one ratified, and a loader that inferred the capacity could never say so.
ag:world a ag:World ; ag:currentVersion ag:version_1 ;
    prov:qualifiedAttribution [ prov:agent <http://example.org/agora/user/dimonina> ; prov:hadRole ag:Sovereign ] .
ag:version_1 a ag:WorldVersion ; ag:versionNumber 1 .


##########  The subject — a thing that is watched, and nothing more  ##########
#  NOT an water:Plant here, and the difference is the point: a plant is a participant in a water
#  economy (it is served by a source, it has a pot size), and this world has no economy for it
#  to participate in. It is a feature of interest — something a sensor reads. The same IRI is
#  an water:Plant over in world/society, because there it is one.

ag:fern a sosa:FeatureOfInterest ;
    ag:localId "fern" .

##########  The board  ##########

#  ONE board, THREE sensors. The ESP32 carries a capacitive probe and a KY-015, and it is one
#  MQTT client with one credential — so it publishes ONE message on ONE topic, and each sensor
#  says which value in it is its own. That is what mqtt:readingPointer is for; the probe states
#  none because `/value` is the default and is what every single-property board already sends.
#
#  All three are sensing:ScheduledProcedure and share ONE command topic, because there is one board and it
#  sleeps once: a cadence is a property of the board, not of a property being measured. The
#  agent aims that channel once, at the tightest cadence any sensor on it asks for.
#
#  Each states the UNIT of what it reports, as a QUDT IRI. That is not decoration on a board
#  like this one: soil moisture 0.183 and air humidity 0.46 are both dimensionless fractions
#  and look identical, while 21.4 is degrees Celsius — three numbers, two dimensions, and
#  before this nothing in the graph said which was which. None of them states a calibration,
#  so all three get scaling:Identity: the firmware scales before it publishes, and what
#  arrives is already the quantity. Stating the unit converts nothing.

#  WHAT IS MOUNTED ON WHAT, in the one vocabulary an agent may hold for it. `sosa:Platform` is
#  *an entity that hosts other entities, particularly Sensors, Actuators, Samplers, and other
#  Platforms*, and `sosa:hosts` is that relation — so a board hosting a part hosting two
#  channels is expressible without inventing a term. See decisions/a-board-is-a-platform.md.
#
#  It is stated HERE rather than read from the wiring because an agent is never given
#  `hardware.ttl` (agent/genesis.py, HARDWARE_FILES) — the enforcement is that the file is not
#  in the container. The wiring says the same thing in its own words: `mc:carries` is now
#  `rdfs:subPropertyOf sosa:hosts`, so a sovereign loading both sees one fact twice and
#  `tests/test_layout.py` refuses a society whose hosting contradicts its hardware.
#
#  `ag:status_led_fern` is deliberately absent. The board carries it and the society has no
#  business knowing: an agent polls sensors, and a thing it can never observe is the
#  sovereign's. What is projected is the hosting of things the society already names.

ag:esp32_fern a sosa:Platform ;
    ag:localId "esp32_fern" ;
    sosa:hosts ag:moisture_sensor_fern , ag:air_sensor_fern ;
    rdfs:comment "The board. One MQTT client, one credential, one wake — which is why its three channels share a topic and a cadence, and why those are facts about IT rather than about any one of them." .

#  The KY-015 itself, which until now the society did not name at all: its two channels were
#  loose sensors with nothing tying them to the part they read. That was the gap issue #51 left
#  open when it split them, and hosting is the thing that closes it.
ag:air_sensor_fern a ssn:System ;
    ag:localId "air_sensor_fern" ;
    ssn:hasSubSystem ag:air_temp_fern , ag:air_humidity_fern ;
    rdfs:comment "One part reporting two properties. A System rather than a Sensor because a sensor observes ONE property here, and this observes none itself — it has the two that do as sub-systems. Not a Platform either: it is hosted and has sub-systems, and nothing is MOUNTED on a KY-015. What the part itself does is produce both values in one frame, which is why they share an instant." .

ag:moisture_sensor_fern a sosa:Sensor , ag:Device ;
    ag:localId "moisture_sensor_fern" ;
    mqtt:onBus ag:local_bus ;
    sensing:senseMode sensing:ScheduledProcedure ;     # keeps a given interval -> its agent gains sensing:Subscribing
    #  NOT ssn:implements sensing:AlarmProcedure — yet. The firmware supports wake-on-crossing
    #  (#151) but this board has not been reflashed with it, and stating the promise before the
    #  hardware keeps it would teach the agent that silence means "nothing crossed" about a board
    #  for which silence still only means "not due yet". Add the line when the board is flashed.
    sensing:monitors ag:fern ;
    sosa:observes water:SoilMoisture ;
    scaling:quantityUnit unit:UNITLESS ;   # a fraction of saturation, 0-1
    mqtt:readingTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/reading" ;
    mqtt:commandTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/command" .

#  The KY-015's two channels. They are sensing:Sensor and NOT devices: they have no mqtt:onBus of
#  their own and mint no credential, because the thing that connects to the broker is the
#  board, which already has one. `ag:air_sensor_fern` in hardware.ttl is the peripheral they
#  read — its pins, its model and its wires are stated there and nothing here repeats them.

ag:air_temp_fern a sosa:Sensor , ag:Device ;
    ag:localId "air_temp_fern" ;
    #  no senseMode here since #96: the clock is the BOARD's, stated once where it
    #  connects — this element rides that wire and shares that answer
    sensing:monitors ag:fern ;
    sosa:observes water:AirTemperature ;
    scaling:quantityUnit unit:DEG_C ;      # the one reading here that is NOT a fraction
    mqtt:readingTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/reading" ;
    mqtt:readingPointer "/temperature" ;
    mqtt:commandTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/command" ;
    # PROJECTED from the wiring, not typed. `dht11:Dht11` states this floor on the class, and an
    # agent is never given hardware.ttl — so it is restated here in the one vocabulary an agent
    # may hold for it. `tests/test_layout.py` holds the two to agreeing.
    ssn-system:hasSystemCapability [
        a ssn-system:SystemCapability ;
        ssn-system:inCondition [ a ssn-system:Condition ; rdfs:label "continuous operation" ] ;
        ssn-system:hasSystemProperty [ a ssn-system:Frequency , schema:PropertyValue ; schema:value 2 ; schema:unitCode unit:SEC ] ] .

ag:air_humidity_fern a sosa:Sensor , ag:Device ;
    ag:localId "air_humidity_fern" ;
    #  no senseMode here since #96: the clock is the BOARD's, stated once where it
    #  connects — this element rides that wire and shares that answer
    sensing:monitors ag:fern ;
    sosa:observes water:AirHumidity ;
    scaling:quantityUnit unit:UNITLESS ;   # relative humidity as a fraction, 0-1
    mqtt:readingTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/reading" ;
    mqtt:readingPointer "/humidity" ;
    mqtt:commandTopic "sensors/moisture_sensor_fern/command" ;
    # PROJECTED from the wiring, not typed. `dht11:Dht11` states this floor on the class, and an
    # agent is never given hardware.ttl — so it is restated here in the one vocabulary an agent
    # may hold for it. `tests/test_layout.py` holds the two to agreeing.
    ssn-system:hasSystemCapability [
        a ssn-system:SystemCapability ;
        ssn-system:inCondition [ a ssn-system:Condition ; rdfs:label "continuous operation" ] ;
        ssn-system:hasSystemProperty [ a ssn-system:Frequency , schema:PropertyValue ; schema:value 2 ; schema:unitCode unit:SEC ] ] .

##########  The agent  ##########
#  No ag:actsFor: with nothing to advance for it, this agent holds no stake — it records.
#  No market:bidsIn and no market:hosts, so no market capability can be derived for it.

ag:fern_agent a ag:Agent ;
    ag:localId "fern" ;
    sensing:polls ag:moisture_sensor_fern , ag:air_temp_fern , ag:air_humidity_fern ;
    mqtt:eventTopic "readings/fern" .

##########  What each agent may re-pick, and how far  ##########
#  A MANDATE: the room this world gives an agent on one of its own settings. Here rather than in
#  its beliefs because latitude GRANTED is a governance fact — what an agent is allowed, not what
#  it wants. The pick inside the range stays private; the range does not.
#
#  Stating one is also what GRANTS the review capability: room to move and the ability to use it
#  are one decision said once, so `capabilities/review/rules.ru` derives review:Reckoning from exactly
#  these triples. An agent with no mandate never arises, which is how a deployment that wants no
#  drift says so — by granting none, rather than by omitting a belief and hoping something
#  notices. The supplier has none: it holds no revisable setting, so there is nothing to give it
#  room on.
#
#  Note what is NOT written here: no ag:hasCapability. See AGENTS.md.

ag:fern_agent review:commits [ review:onTerm sensing:slowSleepS ; review:notBelow 10 ; review:notAbove 900 ] .
